<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?><ns2:section xmlns:ns2="http://rest.cis.illinois.edu" id="45956" href="schedule/2007/spring/COMM/321/45956.xml"><parents><calendarYear id="2007" href="schedule/2007.xml">2007</calendarYear><term id="120071" href="schedule/2007/spring.xml">Spring 2007</term><subject id="COMM" href="schedule/2007/spring/COMM.xml">ZZZ Communications</subject><course id="321" href="schedule/2007/spring/COMM/321.xml">Film Culture</course></parents><sectionNumber>D  </sectionNumber><statusCode>A</statusCode><sectionText>Topic:  Cult Films   This course seeks to examine presentations of deviancy in the cult film.  Characters in cult films are marginalized and constructed as perverse in a variety of ways: as sexual deviants, social outcasts, and criminal perverts.  Cult films also cross into other genres which have been examined for their presentation of others, such as horror films and science fiction.</sectionText><partOfTerm>1</partOfTerm><sectionStatusCode>A</sectionStatusCode><enrollmentStatus>UNKNOWN</enrollmentStatus><startDate>2007-01-16Z</startDate><endDate>2007-05-02Z</endDate><meetings><meeting id="0"><type code="LEC">Lecture</type><start>12:00 PM</start><end>01:20 PM</end><daysOfTheWeek>MW     </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>218</roomNumber><buildingName>Mechanical Engineering Bldg</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="Kosovski" firstName="J">Kosovski, J</instructor></instructors></meeting></meetings></ns2:section>